WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.782
Simple CASE is useful when one expression needs to be mapped to a set of exact values.

00:00:06.782 --> 00:00:13.717
For example, payment status can be Paid, No Charge, Open, Pending Review, or something else.

00:00:13.717 --> 00:00:20.247
We can compare that one field against each expected value and return a corresponding action.

00:00:20.247 --> 00:00:23.447
Paid and No Charge may map to Closed.

00:00:23.447 --> 00:00:25.797
Open may map to Follow up.

00:00:25.797 --> 00:00:28.647
Pending Review may map to Investigate.

00:00:28.647 --> 00:00:31.302
Anything else can fall to ELSE.

00:00:31.302 --> 00:00:35.592
This is similar to a lookup table written directly inside the query.

00:00:35.592 --> 00:00:40.272
Simple CASE is clean when the rule is truly based on exact values.

00:00:40.272 --> 00:00:47.507
It is less suitable for conditions such as amount greater than five thousand, age above sixty-five,

00:00:47.507 --> 00:00:51.162
or status is missing AND severity is High.

00:00:51.162 --> 00:00:54.802
Those are better expressed with searched CASE.

00:00:54.802 --> 00:00:59.592
A practical example is converting coded values to readable labels.

00:00:59.592 --> 00:01:04.787
A university system might map A to Active, G to Graduated, and W to Withdrawn.

00:01:04.787 --> 00:01:11.512
A support system might map ticket codes to display categories.

00:01:11.512 --> 00:01:14.992
Notice what happens when payment status is NULL.

00:01:14.992 --> 00:01:20.482
NULL does not match the named values, so the ELSE branch becomes important.

00:01:20.482 --> 00:01:23.202
In this example, ELSE can label it Status missing.

00:01:23.202 --> 00:01:29.537
Choose simple CASE when you are mapping one field's exact values.

00:01:29.537 --> 00:01:33.098
Choose searched CASE when the rule itself contains conditions.
